/**
\page reference Library reference

Use this reference to select the C++ installation interface or look up a retained
CEL signature. Operation links point to the CEL language book, which defines
numeric behavior, error conditions, and examples.

\section package Package and installer

The CMake package is `cel-std`; its exported target is `cel::std`.
The public header is `<cel/std.hpp>`.
Call cel::stdlib::install with a `cel::op_lookup` before constructing your parser.
See \ref tutorial for a compiled example.

The installer registers persistent ordinary and receiver-call signatures.
The receiver supplies the first operand; no numeric conversions are introduced.
Independent libraries can register disjoint overloads before or after installation.
Install each exact standard-library signature only once per lookup.

\section signatures Retained CEL signatures

Every `f64` maps to C++ `double`. Scalar result types remain `f64`, including
rounding operations. `sin_cos` returns a structural tuple whose C++ adapter is
`std::tuple<double, double>`.

The following table is the entire port, not the book's larger Rust type set:

| CEL signature | Operation contract |
| --- | --- |
| `min(a: f64, b: f64) -> f64` | [min](https://stlab.github.io/cel-rs/cel-book/standard-library.html#min) |
| `max(a: f64, b: f64) -> f64` | [max](https://stlab.github.io/cel-rs/cel-book/standard-library.html#max) |
| `clamp(x: f64, lo: f64, hi: f64) -> f64` | [clamp](https://stlab.github.io/cel-rs/cel-book/standard-library.html#clamp) |
| `abs(x: f64) -> f64` | [abs](https://stlab.github.io/cel-rs/cel-book/standard-library.html#abs) |
| `signum(x: f64) -> f64` | [signum](https://stlab.github.io/cel-rs/cel-book/standard-library.html#signum) |
| `sqrt(x: f64) -> f64` | [sqrt](https://stlab.github.io/cel-rs/cel-book/standard-library.html#sqrt) |
| `floor(x: f64) -> f64` | [floor](https://stlab.github.io/cel-rs/cel-book/standard-library.html#floor) |
| `ceil(x: f64) -> f64` | [ceil](https://stlab.github.io/cel-rs/cel-book/standard-library.html#ceil) |
| `trunc(x: f64) -> f64` | [trunc](https://stlab.github.io/cel-rs/cel-book/standard-library.html#trunc) |
| `fract(x: f64) -> f64` | [fract](https://stlab.github.io/cel-rs/cel-book/standard-library.html#fract) |
| `round_ties_even(x: f64) -> f64` | [round_ties_even](https://stlab.github.io/cel-rs/cel-book/standard-library.html#round-ties-even) |
| `round(x: f64) -> f64` | [round](https://stlab.github.io/cel-rs/cel-book/standard-library.html#round) |
| `sin(x: f64) -> f64` | [sin](https://stlab.github.io/cel-rs/cel-book/standard-library.html#sin) |
| `cos(x: f64) -> f64` | [cos](https://stlab.github.io/cel-rs/cel-book/standard-library.html#cos) |
| `tan(x: f64) -> f64` | [tan](https://stlab.github.io/cel-rs/cel-book/standard-library.html#tan) |
| `asin(x: f64) -> f64` | [asin](https://stlab.github.io/cel-rs/cel-book/standard-library.html#asin) |
| `acos(x: f64) -> f64` | [acos](https://stlab.github.io/cel-rs/cel-book/standard-library.html#acos) |
| `atan(x: f64) -> f64` | [atan](https://stlab.github.io/cel-rs/cel-book/standard-library.html#atan) |
| `atan2(y: f64, x: f64) -> f64` | [atan2](https://stlab.github.io/cel-rs/cel-book/standard-library.html#atan2) |
| `sin_cos(x: f64) -> (f64, f64)` | [sin_cos](https://stlab.github.io/cel-rs/cel-book/standard-library.html#sin-cos) |
| `to_radians(x: f64) -> f64` | [to_radians](https://stlab.github.io/cel-rs/cel-book/standard-library.html#to-radians) |
| `to_degrees(x: f64) -> f64` | [to_degrees](https://stlab.github.io/cel-rs/cel-book/standard-library.html#to-degrees) |
| `hypot(x: f64, y: f64) -> f64` | [hypot](https://stlab.github.io/cel-rs/cel-book/standard-library.html#hypot) |
| `cbrt(x: f64) -> f64` | [cbrt](https://stlab.github.io/cel-rs/cel-book/standard-library.html#cbrt) |
| `powf(x: f64, exponent: f64) -> f64` | [powf](https://stlab.github.io/cel-rs/cel-book/standard-library.html#powf) |
| `exp(x: f64) -> f64` | [exp](https://stlab.github.io/cel-rs/cel-book/standard-library.html#exp) |
| `exp2(x: f64) -> f64` | [exp2](https://stlab.github.io/cel-rs/cel-book/standard-library.html#exp2) |
| `ln(x: f64) -> f64` | [ln](https://stlab.github.io/cel-rs/cel-book/standard-library.html#ln) |
| `log(x: f64, base: f64) -> f64` | [log](https://stlab.github.io/cel-rs/cel-book/standard-library.html#log) |
| `log2(x: f64) -> f64` | [log2](https://stlab.github.io/cel-rs/cel-book/standard-library.html#log2) |
| `log10(x: f64) -> f64` | [log10](https://stlab.github.io/cel-rs/cel-book/standard-library.html#log10) |
| `recip(x: f64) -> f64` | [recip](https://stlab.github.io/cel-rs/cel-book/standard-library.html#recip) |
| `mul_add(x: f64, multiplier: f64, addend: f64) -> f64` | [mul_add](https://stlab.github.io/cel-rs/cel-book/standard-library.html#mul-add) |
| `rem_euclid(x: f64, divisor: f64) -> f64` | [rem_euclid](https://stlab.github.io/cel-rs/cel-book/standard-library.html#rem-euclid) |
| `copysign(magnitude: f64, sign: f64) -> f64` | [copysign](https://stlab.github.io/cel-rs/cel-book/standard-library.html#copysign) |

No `powi`, integer, or `f32` signature is installed. Those parts of the linked
book describe Rust capabilities outside this port.

\section floating Floating-point environment

Build for IEEE 754 binary64 without fast-math assumptions.
The implementation uses C++ standard-library math and preserves the book's
documented NaN, infinity, signed-zero, and rounding behavior. Transcendental
results need not be bit-identical across implementations; compare them using a
tolerance. NaN payloads and signs are unspecified.

`round_ties_even` is independent of the ambient rounding mode and does not
change that mode. The library does not promise to preserve floating-point
exception flags or `errno`.

During evaluation, invalid clamp bounds throw
`std::invalid_argument("invalid clamp bounds")`. Other numeric domain failures
produce the floating-point results documented in the book. Unsupported
signatures are parser compilation errors.
*/
